<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Rhodesia</h1><p class="st-meta">1911 CE – 1964 CE · capital Livingstone; later Lusaka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate on the Zambezi built around the Copperbelt, which became independent Zambia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1911 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> merged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/north-western-rhodesia">North-Western Rhodesia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/north-eastern-rhodesia">North-Eastern Rhodesia</a> into Northern Rhodesia, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livingstone-zambia">Livingstone</a>. In 1924 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> took over administration, though the company kept the mineral rights until 1964.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From the late 1920s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copperbelt">Copperbelt</a> grew into one of the world's leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> producers, drawing African workers whose strikes in 1935 and 1940 were early signs of organised protest. The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a> in 1935. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland</a> kept a protected status under its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> king. Settlers were few but sought amalgamation with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-rhodesia">Southern Rhodesia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The territory was joined to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> in 1953. Opposition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-nkumbula">Harry Nkumbula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-independence-party">United National Independence Party</a> ended the federation, and Northern Rhodesia became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> on 24 October 1964 with Kaunda as president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/northern-rhodesia.jpg" alt="Northern Rhodesia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Greentubing (en:WP talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Northern_Rhodesia_(1939%E2%80%931964).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1911 CE – 1964 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Livingstone; later Lusaka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kenneth Kaunda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Chibemba, Chinyanja, Silozi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Northern Rhodesia Protectorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=northern-rhodesia" aria-label="Northern Rhodesia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5432 2717 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Rhodesia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5752 2973L5629 3000M5752 2973L5863 2875M5752 2973L5720 3066M5752 2973L5842 3096M5752 2973L4997 651M5752 2973L5842 3096"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5629 3000h0M5863 2875h0M5720 3066h0M5842 3096h0M4997 651h0M5842 3096h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5752 2973h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5752 2973h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=northern-rhodesia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
